<p>A related behavior is answering the literal question instead of finding the right solution to the actual problem.
Sometimes the problem is the newb asks how to do something, and experts
answer the question literally rather than stepping back to find the real problem
and suggest a better solution.  Sometimes this is called an <a urlid="xy">XY
Problem</a>: rather than asking about the original problem, they ask about
fixing the bad solution they've already chosen.</p>

<quote>
https://discord.com/channels/267624335836053506/267624335836053506/1550975286080708708
<p><b>newb</b>: is there a way to make a class so that every method is automatically a @classmethod without having to manually type it out for each function?</p>
<p><b>expert1</b>: if you want everything to be a classmethod, maybe you don't want a class</p>
<p><b>expert2</b>: You could use a meta class or a class decorator</p>
<p><b>expert1</b>: wouldn't you agree that likely there is a non-class solution that fits well?</p>
<p><b>expert2</b>: I would</p>
</quote>

<url id="xy" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XY_problem" />


